//OUT="a"

// `brk` ends its block, so the `out "b"` that follows it in the same block can
// never run. Unreachable code is accepted rather than rejected — nothing else
// in the language diagnoses it, and a competitive programmer commenting a line
// out should not have to restructure the loop.

fn main
    while true
        out "a"
        brk
        out "b"
